// `base` in a type that has no base names nothing, and says so.
//
// Both the field form and the call form used to report a fragment — "base access" and "base call outside
// a derived class" — the first naming neither the member nor the type. Two quite different problems
// (there is no base at all / the base has no such member) reported as one non-sentence.
type resource Lonely {
    int32 n;
    public ctor make() { this.n = 1; }
    public fn int32 peek() { return base.n; }        // ERROR: 'Lonely' has no base class
}
fn int main() { Lonely l = Lonely.make(); return l.peek(); }
